the john wick films feature some of the  most satisfying scenes in modern action  cinema  with that in mind here are the most  paused moments in the john wick  franchise  from the ink that shows off john's  violent past to all of the other shots  that are just plain cool  we all knew going into the first john  wick that things would get violent  what we didn't know was just how a  seemingly good-natured guy  learned how to beat someone to death  with a book or kill three men with a  pencil  we got our first glimpse of his dark  side shortly after his beloved dog was  killed  and he decided that he was returning to  his old life before he sets out for the  continental hotel  john got ready for action with a nice  shower revealing that this quiet  suburban widower had a glorious and  ominous full back tattoo  there are a few notable elements to this  piece of ink but the most eye-catching  by far  is the black lettering across the  shoulders which reads fortis fortuna  argue what  that's latin for fortune favors the  brave the entire scene is very shadowy  and goes by rather quickly  so unless you're both familiar with  latin and have great eyesight you're  going to want to pause it to take in the  full effect  once you do you'll see how appropriately  it sets the stage for the coming rampage  as jon will have some pretty good luck  while going directly at his targets  like a wrecking ball meanwhile the guy  who picked on an adorable puppy and then  spends the rest of the movie running  away  is going to have a pretty rough time the  fight scenes in john wicker often hail  to some of the best in modern action  cinema  and justifiably so but they're not the  only thing about these movies that  deserve praise  one element that's often overshadowed is  just how beautifully shot these films  are  for those of you looking for a new  desktop background or phone lock screen  john wick provides plenty of options one  of our favorites is a shot of john  walking away in slow motion from a  burning pile  of money after he takes on a church  that's a friend for criminal operations  the cool guy walking away trope has a  long and proud tradition in action  movies  so much so that the lonely island even  wrote a song about it  john wick's spin on the routine doesn't  involve an explosion  although a burning pile of cash has its  own intensity but what it lacks in  pyrotechnics it makes up for in pure  style  the slow motion the shadows falling  across keanu reeves face  and the flames coming up from behind  wick as he descends deeper into his own  personal hell  all make it well worth it to hammer that  pause button to get your screenshot just  right  keanu reeves isn't always thought of as  the most emotive actor  if you're looking for someone who can  pull off goofy's spaciness or zen khan  he's your guy  but if you want a performance that's  full of emotion and energy you might not  be your first call  but with john wick he proves a folly of  that belief true john has the same quiet  understated intensity that reeves has  brought to plenty of other roles but  that just makes it all the more  satisfying when he finally does snap  and snap he does his first face to face  confrontation with michael nikvis vigo  is one of the most iconic moments of the  entire franchise  thanks to that growling delivery of but  now yeah  i'm taken on back it's a moment so cool  that it was prominently featured in the  trailer and it makes the entire scene  incredibly memorable  it's worth pausing for a couple of  reasons for one we rarely get to witness  reeves and a full-on rage which makes it  pretty easy to believe those rumors that  wick is actually a supernatural creature  also considering that this scene builds  to reeves shelting  so you can either hand over your son  or you can die screaming alongside it  you might need to take a pause just to  calm yourself down for a bit  if you're the type of moviegoer who  checks does the dogdy.com before  watching anything  john wick probably isn't the best choice  for you nevertheless there actually is  plenty in the first movie for dog lovers  to enjoy the most obvious is the fact  that the majority of the film is focused  on a guy  making sure that everyone even remotely  involved with being mean to his puppy  is met with vengeance furthermore the  ending brings him to another furry  creature who needs a little help  the scene of john in the veterinary  hospital provides an emotional climax  that most relationship dramas would be  jealous of  that someone is a pit bull who's  scheduled to be put to sleep but this  soft-hearted assassin isn't about to  just let that happen without doing  anything  it's absolutely worth a pause when jon  lets his new best friend out of his cage  if only because it's one of the best  shots of the dog that we get in the  entire movie  it's not like we had any doubts that he  was a good boy but it's still nice to  get a good long look to know for sure  the first john wick tells us the basics  of the shadowy world of assassins  and their preferred hotels but chapter 2  really kicks that into high gear  as we learn about the markers they're  essentially a physical representation of  a favor owed to someone  while they're small enough to fit in a  pocket they carry a considerable weight  if someone has your marker you can't  refuse their request no matter what it  is  lest you bring the wrath of the entire  assassin well down upon your head with  their typical penchant for drama the  assassins have hidden needles in these  medallions so that they can formally  seal their favors with bloody  thumbprints  two rules that cannot be broken jonathan  no blood on continental grounds and  every marker must be honored  the markers embody the compelling design  sensibility behind the artifacts of this  deadly world and they make us wish that  there was a john wick artbook so that we  could see them in detail  for now we'll have to make do with  porting the few close-up shots that we  get that otherwise go by too fast  for a movie that's primarily about keanu  reeves finding exciting new ways to kill  people  a surprising amount of john wick chapter  2 takes place in and around museums  in particular the villainous santino  seems fond of surrounding himself with  fine arts  which has a nice side effect of  providing viewers with some suitably  dramatic shots  and that's not the only reason for all  that art it's definitely worth giving  the pause button a workout to take a  closer look if you do you might be able  to figure out a little bit of the  symbolism on display  take for instance a scene when john  meets up with santino agreeing to the  favor his marker is demanding  the two of them sit in front of giovanni  fattoris la battaglia de custoza  an 1880 painting depicting a battle from  the third italian war of independence  despite outnumbering their enemies 120  000 to 80 000  the italian army was forced to retreat  leaving behind  thousands of prisoners it's probably not  a stretch to say that santino parking  himself in front of a painting  about how overwhelming numbers aren't  always going to result in a victory  might have some thematic resonance this  was my father's collection i see a  little more than just paint on canvas of  course  saying that the john wick films of great  fight scenes is like saying that water  is wet  these movies are essentially six hours  of non-stop crazy battles with a few  breaks for hanging out with dogs  if you're looking for the highlights of  the highlights they don't get much  better than the hall of mirrors fight in  chapter two we're not sure why santino  even has a hall of mirrors for such a  prolonged throwdown  but since the prevailing concern for the  john wick design team seems to be  how cool does this look that logical  concern doesn't really matter  what does matter is that this scene  provides some of the best visuals in the  series from the battle between john and  the bodyguard played by ruby rose  to the kaleidoscopic fights where john  and his opponents are surrounded by  infinite reflections  not since the matrix revolutions have we  been excited to see reeves duking it out  with infinite identical opponents  the entire sequence is about seven  minutes of pure adrenaline  and virtually every shot is worth  pausing to take it all in  who says gracious violence can't be  absolutely beautiful  despite its unwieldy title john wick  chapter 3 parabellum  doesn't waste a lot of time within 7  minutes jon is beating a giant man to  death with a book  in the middle of the new york public  library and it doesn't slow down from  there  about 15 minutes later he's fighting  people while riding a horse through  midtown manhattan  it's a wild ride the library fight isn't  the kind of scene you pause though  even if it is one you'll want to watch  over and over again the fight scene that  follows however  is one that will have you freezing the  action if only to see how they managed  to pull off the visual effects without  actually killing any of the actors  when a group of assassins corner john in  a warehouse full of antique weapons  that someone has kept incredibly sharp  we get some knife throwing that goes  past brutal and into hilarious territory  this scene demands to be paused for a  couple of reasons first it's pretty fun  to tally up the number of knives that  jon manages to hook at his opponents in  40 seconds  it's 19 if you're curious also you'll  want to marvel at how convincing the  special effects of knives sticking out  of people are  just as our initial glimpse of john's  tattoo in the first film requires  pressing pause to get a good look at it  the scene in parabellum that finds him  arriving at the tarkovsky theater in  hopes of escaping the bounty on his head  demands the same  for one there's a ballerina who has a  similar tattoo proving that while jon  might be the most unstoppable graduate  from the director's training program  he's not the only one this trip  backstage is also full of world building  that goes by just a little too quickly  for obsessive fans the other ballerinas  suffering for their art the group  practicing jujitsu  and even the paintings lining the  director's cozy studio are all crying  out for more attention  the big attraction though is a golden  rosary that john hands over  a ticket taken by the director that's  then used to brand him with an inverted  cross  right on his tattoo of all the moments  that show us the secret societies in  john wick  this is the one that feels the most  insular everyone involved knows exactly  what's going on what's being demanded  and what the price will be as much as we  like digging through the background for  details this is one that we like to slow  down just so that we can piece it all  together  you can never come home again john's  meeting with the elder a mysterious  assassin king who lives in the desert  outside casablanca while dreaming of  elaborate displays of fealty  doesn't exactly go well sure he gets his  excommunicado status eliminated but that  only lasts for about 20 minutes before  he's back on the high table's bad side  and he has to pay a significant price if  you haven't seen this scene let's just  say that jon takes off his wedding ring  the hard way  even by the standards of violence in  this franchise this moment is both  intense and  unexpected partly because it's jon doing  something to himself  rather than to someone else it turns out  that the only person in these movies who  can really take on john wick  is john wick himself so whether it's  your first time watching chapter 3 or  your 40th  you're going to want to take a quick  break right before it happens and take a  deep breath  maybe get yourself a glass of water and  mentally prepare  just have the pause button ready for  when john slides his hand across that  table with a chisel on it  you'll thank us later your choice is  yours  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite stuff are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

